\title{Infinite Games Against Death:\\ Immortality Strategies and the $\omega$--$\omega^2$ Dichotomy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and analyze a transfinite \emph{survival game} between two players: \textbf{Mortal}, whose computational power is captured by a well-ordered set of reachable internal configurations, and \textbf{Eternity}, an adversary who may prolong the contest through transfinitely many ordinal-indexed rounds and seeks Mortal's demise. Each round Mortal survives, it must exhibit a strictly later reachable \emph{moment}; the length of any survivable play is therefore bounded by the order type of Mortal's moment set. We define the \textbf{survival value} of a game as this order type and prove a \emph{Fundamental Theorem}: Mortal can force survival to round $\beta$ if and only if $\beta \le \val(G)$. From this single reduction we derive a sharp dichotomy. A finite deterministic Mortal (moments of order type $\omega$) forces every finite round and forces round $\omega$, but dies exactly at $\omega$; moreover \emph{any} Mortal whose moments order-embed into $\N$ cannot pass $\omega$. A Mortal with bounded nondeterminism (moments of order type $\omega^2$, indexed lexicographically) forces every round $\omega \cdot n$ and forces $\omega^2$, but dies exactly at $\omega^2$. Underlying the jump is a \textbf{refinement principle}: subdividing each moment into an $\omega$-block of sub-moments multiplies the survival value by $\omega$. We interpret these thresholds through the lens of Infinite Time Turing Machines, where $\omega$ and $\omega^2$ are the first two clockable milestones met when climbing from deterministic to boundedly nondeterministic transfinite computation.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} ordinals, order type, well-order, transfinite game, survival value, bounded nondeterminism, infinite time Turing machines, clockable ordinals.

\section{Introduction}

Classical computability measures a machine's power by \emph{what} it can compute. A complementary question asks \emph{how long} a machine can persist under an adversary willing to wait transfinitely long. This is naturally phrased as a game. One player, \textbf{Mortal}, is a bounded computational agent; the other, \textbf{Eternity}, controls a clock indexed by the ordinals and wins the instant Mortal cannot make a legal move. Because time is well-founded --- one may climb ordinals indefinitely but never descend them forever --- Mortal's predicament reduces to a single structural fact about the set of configurations it can reach.

Our contribution is a compact, fully rigorous framework for this game together with a sharp analysis of two natural computational regimes. The framework isolates one invariant, the \textbf{survival value}, and shows that survival is \emph{entirely} governed by it (Theorem~\ref{thm:fundamental}). We then compute the value in two cases:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Finite deterministic computation.} Reachable moments have order type $\omega$; survival is exactly $\omega$ (Section~\ref{sec:finite}), and this bound is intrinsic to any system whose configurations embed into $\N$.
  \item \textbf{Bounded nondeterminism.} Reachable moments have order type $\omega^2$; survival is exactly $\omega^2$ (Section~\ref{sec:nondet}).
\end{enumerate}
The mechanism connecting the two is a general \textbf{refinement principle} (Section~\ref{sec:refine}): replacing each moment by an $\omega$-block of sub-moments multiplies survival by $\omega$. Finally (Section~\ref{sec:ittm}) we interpret $\omega$ and $\omega^2$ as the first two clockable milestones of Infinite Time Turing Machines.

\section{The Survival Game}

\subsection{Ordinals, well-orders, and order type}

We work with \textbf{ordinals}, the canonical representatives of well-order types. A linear order $(X,<)$ is a \textbf{well-order} if every nonempty subset has a least element, equivalently if there is no infinite strictly descending sequence. Every well-order has a unique \textbf{order type} $\type(X,<)$, the ordinal to which it is order-isomorphic. We write $\omega$ for the order type of $(\N,<)$, the least infinite ordinal. Ordinal multiplication is defined so that $\alpha \cdot \beta$ is the order type of $\beta$ copies of $\alpha$ laid end to end; in particular $\omega \cdot \omega = \omega^2$.

Two standard facts drive every proof.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(Order-type comparison.)} For well-orders $A,B$, we have $\type(A) \le \type(B)$ iff there is an order embedding (strictly increasing, order-reflecting injection) $A \hookrightarrow B$.
  \item \textbf{(Initial-segment realization.)} For every ordinal $\beta$, the ordinals below $\beta$, ordered by $<$, form a well-order of type $\beta$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The game and its value}

\begin{definition}[Survival game]
A \emph{survival game} $G$ consists of a set $\Moment(G)$ of \textbf{moments of being alive}, linearly ordered by a relation $<$ that is a well-order. A moment is an internal configuration certifying that Mortal is still alive; the well-order encodes that time advances and never regresses.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Survival value]
The \emph{survival value} of $G$ is the order type of its moments,
\[
  \val(G) := \type\big(\Moment(G), <\big).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Play]
For an ordinal $\beta$, a \emph{play of length $\beta$} is an order embedding
\[
  f : (\{\gamma : \gamma < \beta\}, <) \hookrightarrow (\Moment(G), <),
\]
an assignment of a strictly increasing moment to each round below $\beta$. Mortal \textbf{forces round $\beta$}, written $\Forces(G,\beta)$, if a play of length $\beta$ exists.
\end{definition}

To survive $\beta$ rounds, Mortal must, for each round $\gamma < \beta$, produce a moment $f(\gamma)$ strictly later than all previously used moments. Injectivity forbids reuse; monotonicity encodes the forward flow of time.

\section{The Fundamental Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental Theorem of the survival game]\label{thm:fundamental}
For every survival game $G$ and every ordinal $\beta$,
\[
  \Forces(G,\beta) \iff \beta \le \val(G).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) A play of length $\beta$ is an order embedding of the round set $\{\gamma < \beta\}$, of type $\beta$, into $\Moment(G)$, of type $\val(G)$. By order-type comparison, $\beta \le \val(G)$.

($\Leftarrow$) If $\beta \le \val(G)$, then since $\beta$ is the type of its own round set, order-type comparison provides an order embedding of the round set into $\Moment(G)$; this embedding is a play of length $\beta$, so $\Forces(G,\beta)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monotonicity]
If $\Forces(G,\beta)$ and $\gamma \le \beta$, then $\Forces(G,\gamma)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:fundamental} gives $\beta \le \val(G)$; transitivity gives $\gamma \le \val(G)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:fundamental} again.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Survival below the value]\label{cor:below}
If $\beta < \val(G)$ then $\Forces(G,\beta)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Certain death at the value]\label{cor:death}
If $\val(G) < \beta$ then $\neg\,\Forces(G,\beta)$.
\end{corollary}

Thus $\val(G)$ is the least round Mortal cannot force: survival for all rounds strictly below it, guaranteed death at it and beyond.

\section{Finite Determinism: the $\omega$ Barrier}\label{sec:finite}

\begin{definition}[Finite deterministic game]
Let $\Fin$ be the survival game with $\Moment(\Fin) = \N$ under the usual order: a machine with finite memory whose clock advances one tick per round, with no branching.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\val(\Fin) = \omega$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The order type of $(\N,<)$ is $\omega$ by definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Mortal survives every finite round and forces $\omega$]
For every $n \in \N$, $\Forces(\Fin, n)$; moreover $\Forces(\Fin, \omega)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each finite $n$ satisfies $n < \omega = \val(\Fin)$, so $\Forces(\Fin,n)$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:below}. Since $\omega \le \omega = \val(\Fin)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:fundamental} gives $\Forces(\Fin,\omega)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\omega$ is sharp]
$\neg\,\Forces(\Fin, \omega+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\val(\Fin) = \omega < \omega+1$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:death}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Intrinsic $\omega$ bound]\label{thm:intrinsic}
If the moments of a survival game $G$ order-embed into $(\N,<)$, then $\val(G) \le \omega$, hence $\neg\,\Forces(G,\omega+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An order embedding $\Moment(G) \hookrightarrow \N$ gives $\val(G) \le \type(\N) = \omega$ by order-type comparison. The final clause is Corollary~\ref{cor:death}.
\end{proof}

Embeddability into $\N$ is the order-theoretic signature of finite deterministic behavior; Theorem~\ref{thm:intrinsic} says no such machine survives past $\omega$.

\section{Bounded Nondeterminism: the $\omega^2$ Barrier}\label{sec:nondet}

\begin{definition}[Bounded-nondeterministic game]
Let $\Nd$ be the survival game with $\Moment(\Nd) = \N \times_{\lex} \N$, pairs $(b,t)$ ordered lexicographically: $(b,t) < (b',t')$ iff $b < b'$, or $b=b'$ and $t < t'$. The major coordinate $b$ counts completed $\omega$-blocks; the minor coordinate $t$ counts ticks within the current block. A bounded nondeterministic reset increments $b$ and restarts $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\val(\Nd) = \omega^2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The lexicographic product of two well-orders has order type $\type(\text{minor}) \cdot \type(\text{major})$ in the convention where $\alpha \cdot \beta$ is $\beta$ copies of $\alpha$. Both factors have type $\omega$, so $\val(\Nd) = \omega \cdot \omega = \omega^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nondeterministic Mortal survives every $\omega \cdot n$ and forces $\omega^2$]
For every $n \in \N$, $\Forces(\Nd, \omega \cdot n)$; moreover $\Forces(\Nd, \omega^2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For finite $n$, $\omega \cdot n \le \omega \cdot \omega = \omega^2 = \val(\Nd)$ since $n \le \omega$ and left multiplication by $\omega$ is monotone; Theorem~\ref{thm:fundamental} gives $\Forces(\Nd, \omega \cdot n)$. Since $\omega^2 \le \omega^2$, likewise $\Forces(\Nd, \omega^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\omega^2$ is sharp]
$\neg\,\Forces(\Nd, \omega^2+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\val(\Nd) = \omega^2 < \omega^2+1$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:death}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nondeterminism strictly helps]
$\val(\Fin) < \val(\Nd)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\omega = \omega \cdot 1 < \omega \cdot \omega = \omega^2$, since $1 < \omega$ and left multiplication by the positive ordinal $\omega$ is strictly monotone.
\end{proof}

\section{The Refinement Principle}\label{sec:refine}

\begin{definition}[$\omega$-refinement]
For a survival game $G$, its \emph{$\omega$-refinement} $R(G)$ has $\Moment(R(G)) = \Moment(G) \times_{\lex} \N$, ordered lexicographically with the original moment as the major coordinate. Each original moment is expanded into an $\omega$-block of sub-moments.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Refinement multiplies survival by $\omega$]\label{thm:refine}
$\val(R(G)) = \omega \cdot \val(G).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The lexicographic product $\Moment(G) \times_{\lex} \N$ has order type $\type(\N) \cdot \type(\Moment(G)) = \omega \cdot \val(G)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovering $\omega^2$]
$\val(R(\Fin)) = \omega \cdot \omega = \omega^2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:refine} with $\val(\Fin)=\omega$.
\end{proof}

Bounded nondeterminism is exactly one application of $R$; nothing in the argument is special to $\omega$. Iterating $R$ yields survival values $\omega^3, \omega^4, \dots$, and a limit of finite refinements approaches $\omega^\omega$.

\section{Connection to Infinite Time Turing Machines}\label{sec:ittm}

An \textbf{Infinite Time Turing Machine} (ITTM) executes over ordinal time: ordinary successor steps, and at each limit stage each cell is set to the limit inferior (eventual value) of its earlier contents before continuing from a limit state. ITTMs decide sets far beyond the arithmetic hierarchy and organize a theory of \textbf{clockable} ordinals --- those arising as halting times.

The survival game models how far such a machine clocks before its first structural reckoning.
\begin{itemize}
  \item A \emph{deterministic} ITTM with a finite alphabet that must halt traces, before its first limit intervention, reachable clock values order-isomorphic to an initial segment of $\omega$: precisely $\Fin$, value $\omega$.
  \item Allowing a \emph{bounded} amount of nondeterministic branching lets the machine reset a bounded counter across limit stages, stacking $\omega$-blocks; the reachable clock values realize $\omega^2$: precisely $\Nd$, value $\omega^2$.
\end{itemize}
The ordinals $\omega$ and $\omega^2$ are the first two clockable milestones met when climbing from deterministic to boundedly nondeterministic transfinite computation, pinned down exactly by the sharpness theorems. The refinement principle is the abstract counterpart of adding one more layer of bounded counting across limits.

\section{Discussion and Future Work}

The framework isolates a single invariant, the survival value, and reduces a dynamic transfinite contest to its computation. The dichotomy --- $\omega$ for finite determinism, $\omega^2$ for bounded nondeterminism --- is sharp in both directions, and the refinement principle explains the gap as a single multiplication by $\omega$. Directions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Higher barriers $\omega^n$ and $\omega^\omega$.} Iterating $R$ should yield $\omega^{n+1}$ after $n$ refinements of $\Fin$; a colimit over finite refinements should reach $\omega^\omega$.
  \item \textbf{Explicit strategy synthesis.} Concrete, computable winning schedules (e.g.\ CNF-based encodings of increasing moment sequences) realizing the same values constructively.
  \item \textbf{Genuine ITTM dynamics.} Replace the abstract moment type by an actual ITTM configuration space with the liminf rule and prove the reachable clock values realize the same order types.
  \item \textbf{Determinacy.} Prove that at each threshold either Mortal has a surviving strategy or Eternity has a killing strategy, boundary exactly at the survival value.
  \item \textbf{Clockable ordinals.} Relate survival values systematically to the clockable ordinals of ITTMs.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Survival against an unbounded adversary is governed by one ordinal. Finite deterministic computation reaches $\omega$ and no further; a bounded pinch of nondeterminism reaches $\omega^2$; and a single refinement principle explains why each added layer of bounded structure multiplies survival by a whole factor of infinity. In the infinite game against death, the gap between $\omega$ and $\omega^2$ measures, precisely, the value of a little bit of choice.

